{"id": "section-body-14159", "title": "Retinopathy of Prematurity", "chapterId": "chapter-fourteen", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "EYE DISEASES AND REFRACTIVE ERRORS", "Major Blinding Eye Diseases", "Retinopathy of Prematurity"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-14159", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Retinopathy of Prematurity", "runs": [{"text": "Retinopathy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Prematurity"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1312"}}, {"id": "body-14160", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) is primarily due to unmonitored supplemental oxygen. Major risk factors are Low birthweight (<1500 grams), Gestational age of <30 weeks, High, unregulated oxygen at birth and/or fluctuations in oxygenation and Poor postnatal growth", "runs": [{"text": "Retinopathy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "prematurity"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(ROP)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "primarily"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "due"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "unmonitored"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "supplemental"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "oxygen."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Major"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "risk"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "factors"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Low"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "birthweight (<1500 grams), Gestational age of <30 weeks, High, unregulated oxygen at birth and/or fluctuations in oxygenation and Poor postnatal growth"}]}, {"id": "body-14162", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Prevention", "runs": [{"text": "Prevention"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-14163", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Screenings of infants at risk with appropriate timing of exams and follow up is essential to identify infants in need of treatment as per National RoP Guideline.", "runs": [{"text": "Screenings of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "infants at"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "risk with appropriate timing of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "exams and follow up is essential"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to identify infants in need of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "treatment as per National RoP Guideline."}]}, {"id": "body-14165", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Diagnosis", "runs": [{"text": "Diagnosis"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-14166", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Following pupillary dilation using eye drops, the retina is examined with an indirect ophthalmoscope. The peripheral portions of the retina are pushed into view using scleral depression. International Classification of Retinopathy of Prematurity (ICROP3) developed a diagnostic classification of ROP. These diagnostic classifications define ROP by location (zone), severity (stage) and vascular characteristics in the posterior pole (normal, pre-plus, or plus disease).", "runs": [{"text": "Following"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pupillary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dilation"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "using"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "eye"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "drops,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "retina"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "examined"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "an"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "indirect"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ophthalmoscope."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "peripheral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "portions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of the retina are pushed into view using scleral depression. International Classification of Retinopathy of Prematurity (ICROP3) developed a diagnostic classification of ROP. These diagnostic classifications define ROP by location (zone), severity (stage) and vascular characteristics in the posterior pole (normal, pre-plus, or plus disease)."}]}, {"id": "body-14167", "type": "heading"